1. The two practice sheets and the exam

   blocked:     cylinder, cylinder, cylinder, cone, cone, cone, sphere, sphere, sphere, hemisphere, hemisphere, hemisphere

   interleaved: cylinder, cone, sphere, hemisphere, cone, sphere, hemisphere, cylinder, sphere, hemisphere, cylinder, cone

   exam:        cone, sphere, sphere, cylinder, hemisphere, cone, cylinder, hemisphere

2. How often each sheet makes you choose the formula

   sheet          problems   a new kind of problem, so a choice to make
   blocked             12      4  (33%)
   interleaved         12     12  (100%)
   exam                 8      7  (88%)

   On the blocked sheet the formula is chosen 4 times in 12 problems; the
   other 8 are the same as the problem before. The exam asks for a choice
   on every problem but one. Interleaved practice rehearses what the exam
   tests; blocked practice rehearses using a formula already chosen.

3. A student who never chooses: 'use the formula from the last problem'

   They work out the first problem properly and then stop choosing.

   sheet          right   of   score
   blocked           9   12     75%
   interleaved       1   12      8%
   exam              2    8     25%

   On the blocked sheet the shortcut scores 9 of 12 without ever telling
   a cone from a cylinder, so the sheet feels easy and the score looks good.
   On the interleaved sheet it gets only the first problem, and on the exam
   2 of 8. A blocked sheet's score predicts the exam badly: it measures
   using a formula, and the exam measures choosing one. The interleaved
   sheet's low score is the honest one, and it is low in practice, where
   it costs nothing.

4. The answers, exact

   kind         r   h   volume
   cone         5   3   25 pi
   sphere       3   -   36 pi
   sphere       1   -   4/3 pi
   cylinder     1   7   7 pi
   hemisphere   6   -   144 pi
   cone         2   9   12 pi
   cylinder     4   2   32 pi
   hemisphere   3   -   18 pi
